# The audioif floor. Every gate in this repository runs against this exact
# audioif commit: the components are held to a fixed core the same way
# audioif itself is held to a fixed CircuitPython (its CIRCUITPYTHON_ORACLE).
#
# A floating core makes a component failure unattributable -- you cannot tell
# a rewritten instrument from a moved node underneath it. Moving this pin is
# its own change, with the gates re-run and the goldens re-read, never a
# side effect of some other work.
#
# From 2026-09-02 this pin named an UNRELEASED commit rather than a release
# tag, deliberately: audioif carried four synthio fixes that the accuracy
# program needed -- the CPython press semantics and filter cascade (b420dac),
# a re-pressed finished note being dropped entirely (afb6b71, also reported
# upstream), ring modulation, which the CPython target had never implemented
# (52a1d4e) and which three Phase 2 instruments depend on, and envelope
# reassignment not being live on the CPython target (d84470a). The release
# waited until the bug hunt settled; the floor did not need to.
#
# Moved to d84470a on 2026-09-02. That fix is what makes one circuit shared by
# two voices with different decays behave correctly under CPython, as it
# already did on MicroPython and the oracle: before it, a closed hat struck
# 250 ms after an open hat rang for 400 ms instead of 53 ms. It changes the
# rendered sound of tr808 and cr78, whose hats share a circuit, so both were
# re-listened.
#
# Moved to v0.2.0 on 2026-09-03, the first audioif release that contains the
# four fixes named above, plus the release-tail reclaim (aed9336) and the
# 14-everywhere Note ceiling (63efb13). The pin names a release again for the
# first time since it moved to d84470a; the floor in each pyproject.toml is
# raised to the same release, so floor and pin agree for the first time as
# well. The tag resolves to the commit in the second column.
#
#
# Moved to 2f6cbc3 on 2026-09-07 by the effects program's Phase 1 (anchor
# docs/effects-roadmap.md; audiocomponents#29). That commit is audioif main
# with the eight palette nodes Phase 0's survey asked for and Phase 1 built
# (audioif#37-#44, #47): four FeedbackDelay options, eleven Dynamics options,
# audioshaper.Waveshaper, audiobiquad.Biquad/AllPass, audioladder.Ladder,
# audioverb.Tank, audiomath.SubOctave and audioroute.MidSide, each with its
# own parity probe and golden, all fifteen probes byte-identical on CPython
# and MicroPython. No release: audioif is released once, at Phase 7, when
# the effects work is complete (Brad, 2026-09-06), so this pin names a commit
# and the pyproject floors stay at v0.2.0 until then. This is also the first
# floor to carry the 64-voice ceiling (audioif 8f8b10d); the three
# instruments the ceiling moves at the gate's material are retired to
# REBUILT with Brad's listen of 2026-09-06 as the blessing (audioif#31).
#
# Moved to cebb7ca on 2026-09-15, for audiomodal.Bank. `acoustickit` imports
# it, so the floor had to carry it or the kit could not be built at all -- CI
# installs audioif from this pin and nothing else. Still no release, for the
# reason above: audioif is released once, at Phase 7.
#
# Moved to 3388df4 on 2026-09-17, in the effects program's Phase 4 fix round,
# on Brad's ruling of the same day: a toolkit defect is fixed the moment it is
# found and the phase pins to the merge commit, the version to be named later.
# That commit is audioif PR #86: the CPython mixer twin scales a voice in
# float32 as the kernel does (audioif#84 - CPython and the native builds now
# agree at fractional voice levels, where a sparse +-1 LSB separated them); no
# shared kernel fuses a multiply-add (audioif#79 - proven on hardware the same
# day: the P4 and the S3 render identical digests on all fifteen float nodes,
# where six biquad rows split at cebb7ca); a looping sample shorter than one
# packed word is refused at play() instead of hanging the native mixer
# (audioif#85); and a Splitter takes a block bigger than its ring in pieces
# instead of writing over itself (audioif#87). What still separates a board
# from a desktop is audiocomponents#75: a filter setting a class derives in
# Python float, which is single precision on an ESP32.
#
# Moved to 977ef26 the same evening, under the same ruling: audioif PR #93
# fixes two upstream CircuitPython 10.3.0 defects in audiospeed.SpeedChanger on
# the MicroPython and CPython targets as documented deviations - the rate
# rounds to its Q16 step instead of truncating (audioif#92) and the phase
# carries across a source buffer instead of restarting (audioif#91) - found by
# Bitcrusher's fix round, the only class that uses the node; PR #90 adds the
# CircuitPython copy-manifest lines audiomodal needed. Proven on both boards:
# the hold probe matches the CPython twin on all fifty lines on the P4, the S3
# and desktop MicroPython, and nothing else moved from 3388df4.
#
# Moved to e3b95e7 on 2026-09-17 late evening, same ruling: audioif PR #98
# adds audioshaper.SampleHold (audioif#97), an exact sample-and-hold of
# audioif's own, because a SpeedChanger down-then-up pair can never be made
# reciprocal at Q16 rate resolution - the Phase 4 gate audit traced
# Bitcrusher's slow flange (one sample per ~190k frames) to the node. Nothing
# that existed changed: every verify_dsp row is byte-identical, audiospeed is
# untouched. Board proof of the new node is owed by Phase 4's board run.
#
# Moved to 9018e35 on 2026-09-18, same ruling: the CPython twin's mixer voice
# now borrows its source node's buffer the way the native one does
# (MixerVoice.c stores a pointer at play(); the twin copied). Saturation's
# coupling-pole charge pulls the wet chain behind an attached voice, so on
# every native build and both boards the voice mixed the settled block and on
# CPython it mixed the bang. No native C changed - the boards stay at the
# e3b95e7 firmware, which is DSP-identical - and verify_dsp reads 49/0/0 with
# every earlier row byte-identical. Also in the range since e3b95e7: docs and
# tests only (the Waveshaper headroom note, audioif_util.float32, the
# soundtrack render gate, the upstream PR material, the re-pinned oracle).
#
# 2026-09-21: moved to 02b1841 (audioif PR #107), because this repository's
# stable output needs it. A Component's `output` is an `audioroute.Port` now -
# a zero-copy wire whose identity never changes, so a Rack, a hand-wired Mixer
# and the audio pump all hear a knob that re-points the graph (Mix from zero,
# Phaser's stages, Distortion's Character). On the old pin that node does not
# exist, `_component.PORT` falls back to None, and the suite is green with
# `TheOutputPortIsAWire` SKIPPING, planted fault included: `OK (skipped=6)`
# where it should read `OK (skipped=2)`. The same merge brings the pump's
# portable engine (`audiopump`: the block pull off the interpreter thread, the
# push ring, the event queue the instruments' `scheduled()` writes to, the
# tap), the pump lock as port hooks that default to no-ops, and the lock
# audit's fixes under every node. No DSP changed: `verify_dsp` reads 49/0/0,
# every parity gate is the one already published, and the 65 digest cells and
# the 249-patch smoke are identical with and without the port on all three
# interpreters. Re-run here at the pin: the whole suite, `Ran 1519 tests`.
#
